WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.870
Many memory-safety problems in other
languages appear around arrays,

2
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:05.522
strings, buffers, and indexing.

3
00:00:05.602 --> 00:00:09.646
Rust provides safer abstractions for
collections, strings, slices, and iteration.

4
00:00:09.726 --> 00:00:11.298
Bounds checks, ownership rules,

5
00:00:11.378 --> 00:00:15.029
and borrowing rules remove many classes of
direct memory misuse in safe code.

6
00:00:15.109 --> 00:00:19.153
But developers still make design choices
about how data is accessed and interpreted.

7
00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:22.153
Direct indexing can panic when length
assumptions are wrong.

8
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:26.108
Sometimes indexing is acceptable because
the code has already proven the length.

9
00:00:26.188 --> 00:00:29.951
In other cases, iterator-based patterns or
checked access express intent more

10
00:00:30.031 --> 00:00:30.480
clearly.

11
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.492
Defensive Rust code makes assumptions
visible instead of hiding them in a position

12
00:00:34.572 --> 00:00:37.043
that might become invalid when input shape
changes.

13
00:00:37.123 --> 00:00:38.246
Rust strings are UTF-8,

14
00:00:38.326 --> 00:00:41.471
so code should distinguish text operations
from byte operations.

15
00:00:41.551 --> 00:00:43.966
A character boundary is not always a byte
boundary,

16
00:00:44.046 --> 00:00:47.023
and the meaning of length depends on what
the code is measuring.

17
00:00:47.103 --> 00:00:51.090
Parsing, slicing, and validation should be
explicit about whether the data is text,

18
00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:53.417
bytes, tokens, fields, or a structured
format.

19
00:00:53.497 --> 00:00:55.238
Slices are borrowed views into data.

20
00:00:55.318 --> 00:00:58.407
They are powerful because they let code
operate without copying,

21
00:00:58.487 --> 00:01:01.856
but the relationship between the slice and
the owner must remain clear.

22
00:01:01.936 --> 00:01:05.699
Secure design avoids hidden assumptions
about length, encoding, delimiters,

23
00:01:05.779 --> 00:01:06.565
and input shape.

24
00:01:06.645 --> 00:01:08.836
The goal is not to avoid collections or
slices;

25
00:01:08.916 --> 00:01:11.780
it is to make access patterns match the
actual data contract.
